<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chalukya dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chalukyas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chalukya dynasty ruled large parts of southern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> between the 6th and 12th centuries. The Chalukyas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-i">Pulakeshin I</a> around 543, dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> until the Rashtrakutas overthrew them in 753; the Western Chalukyas of Kalyani revived their power from 973 to about 1189, while an Eastern Chalukya line ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a>. Their temples at Badami, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aihole">Aihole</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pattadakal">Pattadakal</a> are landmarks of Indian architecture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty.jpg" alt="Chalukya dynasty" width="320" height="296" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Map created from DEMIS Mapserver, which are public domain. Koba-chan. Reference: [1] Borders by Rawn3012 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chalukyas_at_their_height.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Chalukyas ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> for over 600 years as three related but separate dynasties: the Chalukyas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a>, and the sibling lines of Kalyani (Western Chalukyas) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-chalukyas">Eastern Chalukyas</a>). Most historians hold that the founders at Badami were natives of the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> region. Their inscriptions call them Harithiputras of the Manavyasa gotra, the lineage of their early overlords, the Kadambas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banavasi">Banavasi</a>, whose territory they took over.</p><p>Later Chalukya court poets told that the founder sprang from the water of oblation in the palm of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a>. An inscription of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-vi">Vikramaditya VI</a> says the family came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a>, a northern origin that Kamath and Moraes link to a common practice in the 11th century of tying southern royal lines to northern kingdoms. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> records themselves are silent about Ayodhya.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The dynasty was established by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-i">Pulakeshin I</a> in 543, who took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Vatapi</a> (modern Badami) as capital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a> commanded the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> and halted the southward march of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada</a>. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aihole">Aihole</a> inscription of 634 he divided his empire into three Maharashtrakas of 99,000 villages each, and the Chinese traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> visited his court. In 642 the Pallava king Narasimhavarman attacked and temporarily occupied Badami, and Pulakeshin II is presumed to have died fighting.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a> had conquered the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> in 616 and made his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kubja-vishnuvardhana">Kubja Vishnuvardhana</a> viceroy there; after Pulakeshin's death the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a> viceroyalty became an independent kingdom. The Rashtrakutas eclipsed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> line in the mid-8th century, and for two centuries the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-chalukyas">Eastern Chalukyas</a> mostly accepted their dominance, gaining a measure of independence under Bhima I (c. 892-921). The Chalukyas revived in 973 as the Western Chalukyas, ruling from Kalyani until the end of the 12th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Badami Chalukyas developed a style called Chalukyan or Karnata Dravida architecture. Nearly a hundred rock-cut and structural monuments survive in the Malaprabha basin, concentrated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aihole">Aihole</a>, Badami, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pattadakal">Pattadakal</a> and Mahakuta and built of reddish-golden sandstone. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> took its place beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> in inscriptions, and Ravikirti wrote the Aihole inscription as a classical poem.</p><p>Under the Western Chalukyas Vijnaneshwara wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitakshara">Mitakshara</a>, a work on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> law, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/someshvara-iii">Someshvara III</a> compiled an encyclopaedia, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manasollasa">Manasollasa</a>. The Chalukyas supported several faiths, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> was prominent in the early period. The dynasty marked a golden age in the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> and a shift from small kingdoms to large empires in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-india">South India</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chalukya dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Chalukyas</b> of Badami rose in the mid-sixth century in the valley of the Malaprabha River in northern Karnataka, a region previously dominated by the Kadamba dynasty of Banavasi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its kings underwent a process of Indianisation to consolidate and magnify their rule, adopting the devaraja idea of the god-king, Sanskrit as the language of the gods and the script of the Pallavas and <b>Chalukyas</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chenla">Chenla · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>On land the Cholas dominated the Western <b>Chalukyas</b> by war and tribute, fought the Eastern Gangas of Kalinga and the Sinhala kings of Rohana, and from 1150 faced constant warfare with the Pandyas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chola-empire">Chola Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Beyond this core the emperors ruled through feudatory dynasties, the samantas, who governed their own lands, paid tribute and sent troops; among them were the Chahamanas of Shakambhari, the Guhilas, the Chandela dynasty, the Kalachuris and the <b>Chalukyas</b> of Saurashtra.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The family rose with the service of Ereyanga, a prominent <b>Chalukya</b> commander, and his sons Ballala I and Vishnuvardhana.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hoysala-empire">Hoysala Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the eleventh century the Chola king Rajendra I sent an expedition to the Ganges around 1021–1023 that claims to have defeated Mahipala I, and later the Kalachuris and the <b>Chalukyas</b> of Kalyani raided Pala territory.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pala-empire">Pala Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
